Ladybird measures the first grapheme when overflow wrapping needs a
minimum width for float placement or for a line after a forced break.
The glyph-position fast path applies only when the glyph boundary is
the first grapheme boundary. Otherwise, the existing shaping fallback
measures that grapheme, preserving its joining context.

Chrome 157.0.8103.78 and WebKit 26.5 keep a fitting character beside
a float in the tested overflow-wrapping cases. Blink’s LineBreaker::
BreakText() calls ShapingLineBreaker::ShapeLine(), which reshapes pieces
at unsafe shaping boundaries. WebKit’s InlineContentBreaker uses
midWordBreak() and TextUtil::breakWord() to measure candidate prefixes.

Gecko’s nsTextFrame::Reflow() calls gfxTextRun::BreakAndMeasureText(),
which tracks character-cluster boundaries and measures ligature pieces.
Firefox 151.0 differs in these float cases: It places the float on the
first line but moves the tested text below it. Ladybird follows the
measured Chrome and WebKit behavior for these cases.

Plain word-break:break-all doesn’t use this overflow-wrapping estimate.
Its explicit soft-wrap opportunities use separately measured prefixes.
The correction covers overflow-wrap:anywhere, overflow-wrap:break-word,
and word-break:break-word, without changing ordinary word wrapping.
